<binaryimage arch="ARM:LE:32:v8:default">
<!--
    RE-friction round 12 case, need `arm-neon-zero-initialization`
    (challenge 68d40081224c0ec5dcedc2d2, ARM 32-bit ELF `trap`,
    `_start_main`).

    P3 dataflow, before ActionLaneDivide.  A SLEIGH constructor that ends in
    `export *[const]:N v` is a DYNAMIC constant-space export, and it lowers to
    `LOAD(const, v)`.  The defining property of the constant space is that an
    address IS its own value, which is the identity `RuleLoadVarnode`
    (ruleaction.cc:4258) applies when `v` is already a constant Varnode.  The
    identity does not stop holding when `v` is a temporary, but nothing applied
    it there, and `RuleCollapseConstants` cannot turn a 16-byte `v` into a
    constant at all (`PcodeOp::isCollapsible` rejects any output wider than a
    `uintb`).  So `vmov.i32 q8,#0` (ARMneon.sinc:549, whose body is
    `tmp:16 = 0; export *[const]:16 tmp`) survived as a LOAD through a pointer,
    `ActionLaneDivide` sliced that LOAD into one word-sized read per lane at
    `v`, `v+4`, `v+8`, `v+0xc`, and the filed output was

        v2 = *(unsigned int *)(0 + 4);
        v3 = *(unsigned int *)(0 + 8);
        v4 = *(unsigned int *)(0 + 0xc);
        v1 = 0;

    Lane 0 needs no offset and folded correctly; the other three read near-null
    memory.  Offsetting a constant-space address changes the value rather than
    selecting a word of it, so folding those three where they stand would have
    printed the literals 4, 8 and 0xc instead.

    The fix rewrites `out:N = LOAD(const, p:S)` to `COPY p` (N == S), to
    `SUBPIECE(p, 0)` (N &lt; S) or to `INT_ZEXT p` (N &gt; S) before the lanes are
    cut, so each lane is split off the VALUE.

    Bytes: hand-encoded A32, the same body as the vendored fixture
    `kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/neonimm_arm_le32`.  `neon_init` fills a
    32-byte stack buffer with two NEON immediates and passes its address to
    `sink`, stubbed `bx lr`; without that call the whole initialization is dead
    code and neither rendering reaches the output.

    The NON-ZERO immediate `vmov.i32 q8,#1` is here on purpose and is the
    regression that the witness binary cannot carry.  The obvious wrong fix is
    to widen `RuleLoadVarnode` so it folds a 16-byte const-space load; that
    works only because this need's value is zero, and would silently TRUNCATE
    every other `simdExpImm_16` arm (ARMneon.sinc:556, 574, 596, ...), none of
    which appears in the witness.  Pass 1 pins all four lanes of `#1` at 1.

    Pass 1 (default) asserts the fix; pass 2 (`option constspaceload off`) pins
    the upstream rendering, which is the bug.  Assertion counts span both
    passes.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x10004">
00482de90db0a0e120d04de25100c0f20d10a0e1cd0a41f45020c0f2cd2a41f4
0d00a0e1f4ffffeb0bd0a0e10088bde8
</bytechunk>
<!-- The callee, stubbed `bx lr`. -->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x10000" readonly="true">1eff2fe1</bytechunk>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>map function r0x10000 sink</com>
  <com>map function r0x10004 neon_init</com>
  <com>lo fu neon_init</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option constspaceload off</com>
  <com>lo fu neon_init</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="constspaceload #1: the non-zero immediate vmov.i32 q8,#1 folds to all four of its 32-bit lanes, default pass only" min="4" max="4">v[0-9]+ = 1;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="constspaceload #2: the zero immediate vmov.i32 q9,#0 becomes two whole 8-byte zero slots, default pass only" min="2" max="2">unsigned long long v[0-9]+; // stack - 0x1</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="constspaceload #3: only the option-off pass reads near-null memory for the lanes that need an offset" min="3" max="3">\*\)\(0 \+ (?:4|8|0xc)\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="constspaceload #4: only the option-off pass leaves the immediate as a 16-byte pointer-shaped value" min="1" max="1">_0_16_ \+ 0xc</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="constspaceload #5: the buffer still escapes to the callee in both passes" min="2" max="2">sink\(&amp;v[0-9]+,&amp;Stackfffffff8\);</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
